#N canvas 40 60 1280 1150 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 22\ \ \ Linear\ prediction\ \ -\ \ a\ neuron\ guesses\ the\ next\ sample\ (1960\ /\ 1970) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1120 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1120 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1120 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 One linear neuron listens to the last 8 samples and guesses the next one \, learning from every mistake (the delta rule \, normalised). What it learns is the resonance of the sound - and its mistakes are what it could not foresee., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 1020 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ guessing\ the\ future 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 Can one neuron hear the future? Give it the last 8 samples of a sound and ask it to guess the next one. The guess is a weighted sum - a linear neuron \, the ADALINE of Widrow and Hoff (1960). Rojas (9.3.4) calls it an autoregressive model: x_t = a1 x_t-1 + ... + a8 x_t-8 + the unpredictable rest., f 52;
#X text 20 233 IT LEARNS WHILE IT LISTENS: after every sample it compares its guess with what really came and nudges each weight by mu x error x the input on that weight - the delta rule of Widrow and Hoff (LMS). Some 48000 small lessons per second., f 52;
#X text 20 329 NORMALISED: the nudge is divided by k = the loudness of the last 8 samples (x[n-1]^2 + ... + x[n-8]^2 \, or 8 x the average power of the last 20 ms if that is bigger). Rojas 8.1: along the input direction the error is a parabola whose curvature is that loudness. A step of 1/curvature fixes the error in one go \, beyond 2/curvature every step overshoots more than it corrects and the weights explode. Divided by k this becomes mu = 1 and mu = 2 \, for any sound., f 52;
#X text 20 485 WHAT IT LEARNS: a sine is perfectly predictable (two weights would do). A vowel is predictable between the pulses of the voice - the neuron learns its resonances (the formants) and the error is what is left: the pulses. Noise can hardly be predicted at all., f 52;
#X text 20 581 THIS IS LPC \, linear predictive coding (Itakura \, Atal \, around 1970). GSM phones sampled the voice at 8 kHz and sent 8 such coefficients and a little error instead of it - here too the neuron runs slower than the sound (6 kHz) \, so its 8 weights span 1.3 ms. Turned around - a buzz through 1 / (1 - w1 z^-1 - ... - w8 z^-8) - the learned resonances sing on another source: cross-synthesis \, as in Paul Lansky's Idle Chatter (1985)., f 52;
#X text 20 737 A LINEAR NEURON: no curve after the sum. Lessons 4 to 8 added the curve to decide and to bend \, to predict a sound the straight sum is the classic answer - the best possible one when the sound is a resonance driven by noise., f 52;
#X obj 410 96 cnv 1 420 190 empty empty WHAT\ THE\ NEURON\ HEARS 8 12 0 13 #e3f0fb #1d3557 0;
