#X obj 410 890 cnv 1 860 226 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 430 916 1) Listen to the input \, then to the error. Sine: the error dies away within milliseconds - eight weights are enough to know a sine forever., f 58;
#X text 430 967 2) Melody: each new note leaves a click of error \, then silence - the neuron re-learns every note. Small mu: slow re-learning \, mu near 1: almost instant., f 58;
#X text 430 1018 3) Vowel: the error is a thin buzz - the resonances are gone \, the pulses are left (the neuron whitens the sound). Switch vowels and watch the learned peaks move., f 58;
#X text 850 916 4) Noise: nothing to predict - the error is as loud as the input and the weights wander. Raising mu makes the guess worse \, not better., f 58;
#X text 850 967 5) Cross: on a vowel switch learning off (freeze) \, listen to cross and move the buzz pitch - the frozen filter sings the vowel at any pitch., f 58;
#X text 850 1018 6) Volume low \, push mu above 2: each step overshoots and grows \, the weights slam into their limits. Back below 2 and it recovers., f 58;
#X connect 71 0 72 0;
#X connect 71 0 73 0;
